15.7 Expected result

You might see something like:

50 packets transmitted, 44 received, 12% packet loss

followed by:

==========================================
MedRoute SDN QoS Measurements
==========================================
Packets Transmitted : 50
Packets Received    : 44
Packet Loss         : 12.0 %
Minimum RTT         : 20.5 ms
Average RTT         : 21.2 ms
Maximum RTT         : 24.1 ms
Individual RTTs     : [...]
==========================================

Do not expect exactly 10% loss.

We configured:

s1 -- 5% loss -- s2 -- 5% loss -- s4

and ping traffic also has a return journey. Loss events are probabilistic, so the observed end-to-end ping loss can differ considerably from either individual link's configured 5%.

That's actually useful: we're measuring the resulting network behavior rather than just copying the configured loss= value.

One important thing to check

Your controller may currently choose:

Route 10.0.0.1 -> 10.0.0.2 : [1, 3, 4]

instead of:

[1, 2, 4]

because both paths currently have the same graph weight:

$$ 1+1=2 $$

If it chooses [1,3,4], you may get:

Packet Loss : 0.0 %

because that path has loss=0.

That is not a Step 15 failure.

In fact, this exposes exactly the limitation we're about to solve:

CURRENT ROUTING

           weight 1
       s2 ───────── s4
      /              |
     /               |
   s1                h2
     \
      \
       s3 ───────── s4
           weight 1

Controller only sees:
path cost = 2
path cost = 2

But the real network actually has:

Path 1
s1 → s2 → s4
Low delay
Higher loss

Path 2
s1 → s3 → s4
Higher delay
Lower loss

The controller doesn't understand those QoS differences yet.

That's the entire reason we're building the cost engine.

Where we are now

After this step, our measurement foundation is:

Metric	Status
OpenFlow packets/bytes	✅
Traffic rate	✅
RTT/latency	✅
Packet loss	🔄 Step 15
Jitter	Next
Utilization	Upcoming
Normalization	Upcoming
Multi-metric SDN cost	Upcoming
Dynamic cost-based routing	Upcoming
AI/ML	❌ Removed

The final goal is not simply:

$$ ShortestPath = minimum\ hops $$

It becomes:

$$ \boxed{ BestPath=\arg\min_P\sum_{e\in P} Cost(e) } $$

with something like:

$$ \boxed{ Cost(e)= w_DD_e+ w_JJ_e+ w_LL_e+ w_UU_e } $$

So a two-hop path with terrible loss may eventually be rejected in favor of another two-hop path with better healthcare-network QoS.

